{"name":"Rumba Red","hex":"#902a40","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rumba","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slow-paced Cuban partner dance in 4:4 time."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dance the rumba."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rumba"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rumba","blocks":[{"text":"The term rumba may refer to a variety of unrelated music styles. Originally, \"rumba\" was used as a synonym for \"party\" in northern Cuba, and by the late 19th century it was used to denote the complex of secular music styles known as Cuban rumba. Since the early 20th century the term has been used in different countries to refer to distinct styles of music and dance, most of which are only tangentially related to the original Cuban rumba, if at all. The vague etymological origin of the term rumba, as well as its interchangeable use with guaracha in settings such as bufo theatre, is largely responsible for such worldwide polysemy of the term. In addition, \"rumba\" was the primary marketing term for Cuban music in North America, as well as West and Central Africa, during much of the 20th century, before the rise of mambo, pachanga and salsa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Bailarines_de_rumba_cubana_en_la_plaza_de_los_trabajadores_de_Camag%C3%BCey%2C_Cuba.jpg/330px-Bailarines_de_rumba_cubana_en_la_plaza_de_los_trabajadores_de_Camag%C3%BCey%2C_Cuba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumba","wikidata":"Q21684437"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cardinal.jpg/500px-Cardinal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cardinal.jpg/500px-Cardinal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACardinal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Cardinal_Th%C3%A9odore_Adrien_Sarr_2.JPG/500px-Cardinal_Th%C3%A9odore_Adrien_Sarr_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Cardinal_Th%C3%A9odore_Adrien_Sarr_2.JPG/500px-Cardinal_Th%C3%A9odore_Adrien_Sarr_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACardinal_Th%C3%A9odore_Adrien_Sarr_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Magdalena_Frackowiak.jpg